<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  CHANGE DETECTION. This file is the reason this module is not a port of the other
  three connectors.

  WooCommerce, PrestaShop and OpenCart all hang change detection off a "product was
  saved" hook. On Magento that approach does not fail — it DRIFTS, which is worse,
  because a drifting index looks healthy. Every one of these changes what a shopper
  sees with no product model saved and no save event dispatched:

    catalog price rules      applied in bulk from cron; rewrites catalogrule_product_price
    tier / group prices      written straight to catalog_product_entity_tier_price
    MSI reservations         order placement writes inventory_reservation
    bin/magento import       resource-level writes for speed; no per-row event
    direct SQL, restores     invisible
    store-view value edits   only the EAV value tables change
    website (re)assignment   only catalog_product_website changes
    category (re)assignment  only catalog_category_product changes — facets move,
                             the product row does not

  SO WE DECLARE OUR OWN VIEW rather than reusing one. Two rejected alternatives,
  both of which look cheaper:

  (a) Poll `updated_at`. Wrong in exactly the same ways — a catalogrule or category
      write does not bump it — and it cannot represent a delete at all.

  (b) Piggyback on `catalogsearch_fulltext_cl`. Tempting and a trap.
      `indexer_clean_all_changelogs` runs every 5 minutes and clears rows below the
      OWNING view's version pointer. The fulltext indexer would delete rows we had
      not read yet: silent loss, on a five-minute cadence, invisible until a
      merchant notices stale results.

  `nitrosearch_product_cl` is OURS. Magento walks it from OUR `mview_state` row,
  in batches, and trims only what we have consumed.

  group="indexer" IS SUFFICIENT AND WE DECLARE NO INDEXER. `Mview\Processor::update()`
  iterates views by group loaded from THIS FILE, not from the indexer registry, and
  `indexer_update_all_views` runs it every minute. Declaring an indexer would put a
  row in the merchant's Index Management screen that they can set to "Update on Save",
  which would silently stop our subscription — a footgun with no upside.

  THE COST OF THAT CHOICE, STATED: because we have no indexer,
  `bin/magento indexer:set-mode schedule` cannot reach us, so the view has to be
  subscribed explicitly. That is what `Setup\Patch\Schema\SubscribeMview` and
  `bin/magento nitrosearch:subscribe` are for, and why `nitrosearch:status` reports
  the subscription state rather than assuming it — a module whose triggers were never
  created looks perfectly healthy and syncs nothing.

  THE TABLE LIST IS INHERITED FROM CORE, NOT INVENTED. The first twelve are exactly
  what `Magento_CatalogSearch`'s own mview.xml subscribes to, read out of the vendor
  tree rather than from documentation: it is Magento's own answer to "what changes
  what a search index must show", and deriving our own would be a worse version of a
  question core has already answered.

  TWO ARE ADDED DELIBERATELY, and both are additions to core's list rather than
  copies of it — core's mview.xml does NOT carry either, which was checked rather
  than assumed:

    catalog_product_entity_tier_price  tier and customer-group prices, which mass
                                       tooling writes directly and which change the
                                       price a shopper is shown
    cataloginventory_stock_item        MSI mirrors the default source into this
                                       legacy table, which covers the single-source
                                       case most Open Source stores actually run

  Subscriptions carry an explicit `entity_column`, because the tables key the product
  differently — `entity_id` on the entity and EAV tables, `product_id` on the link,
  website, rule-price and stock tables. Getting one wrong does not error; it files
  the WRONG id into the changelog, and the wrong product gets re-indexed while the
  right one goes stale.
-->
<config xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:Mview/etc/mview.xsd">
    <view id="nitrosearch_product" class="NitroSearch\Search\Model\Mview\Action" group="indexer">
        <subscriptions>
            <!-- The product itself: create, delete, status, visibility, type. -->
            <table name="catalog_product_entity" entity_column="entity_id"/>

            <!-- The five EAV value tables. A store-view name or description edit
                 touches only these. -->
            <table name="catalog_product_entity_datetime" entity_column="entity_id"/>
            <table name="catalog_product_entity_decimal" entity_column="entity_id"/>
            <table name="catalog_product_entity_int" entity_column="entity_id"/>
            <table name="catalog_product_entity_text" entity_column="entity_id"/>
            <table name="catalog_product_entity_varchar" entity_column="entity_id"/>

            <!-- Composite membership. These fire with the CHILD's id, which is why
                 the action fans out to parents — a configurable's price changes when
                 a child's does, and the parent is what is indexed. -->
            <table name="catalog_product_super_link" entity_column="product_id"/>
            <table name="catalog_product_bundle_selection" entity_column="product_id"/>
            <table name="catalog_product_link" entity_column="product_id"/>

            <!-- Scope and facets. -->
            <table name="catalog_product_website" entity_column="product_id"/>
            <table name="catalog_category_product" entity_column="product_id"/>

            <!-- Catalog price rules, applied with no product save — the case a
                 save-hook module cannot see.

                 MEASURED, AND IT IS PARTIAL COVERAGE. Both halves were run against
                 a real 2.4.8 store rather than reasoned about:

                   deleting 36 rule-price rows for 3 products
                                                  -> 36 changelog rows. CAUGHT.
                   a full `indexer:reindex catalogrule_rule` that restored those
                   same 36 rows                   -> ZERO changelog rows. MISSED.

                 So incremental rule changes reach us and a FULL RULE REINDEX does
                 not: that path writes through a temporary table rather than row by
                 row, and a trigger cannot see a write that never touches the live
                 table. This subscription is still worth having — it is the
                 difference between catching most rule activity and none — but it
                 must not be described as complete coverage, and the README does not
                 describe it that way.

                 THE FULL WALK IS THE CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT, not this. That is the
                 same stance the PrestaShop connector takes about its own long hook
                 list, and this measurement is why it is the right one. -->
            <table name="catalogrule_product_price" entity_column="product_id"/>

            <!-- Added beyond core's list; see the header. -->
            <table name="catalog_product_entity_tier_price" entity_column="entity_id"/>
            <table name="cataloginventory_stock_item" entity_column="product_id"/>
        </subscriptions>
    </view>
</config>
